{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-39205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=15,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v)}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u),$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u) & =\\frac{1}{2}(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{3}{4}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v) & =\\frac{3}{4}(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}((-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(3+3+4+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v)} & =\\frac{(\\frac{15}{2})}{9}=\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-39205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=17,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (2u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v)}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $u\\cdot (2u),$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (2u) & =2(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{1}{3}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v) & =\\frac{1}{3}({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((0)\\cdot (1)+3\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0-12+4-10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{u\\cdot (2u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v)}=\\frac{34}{-6}=-\\frac{17}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-40108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "-\\frac{16}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{17}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{18}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{17}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot v=-1,$ then $\\frac{(5u)\\cdot v}{v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v)}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $(5u)\\cdot v,$ we multiply $u\\cdot v$ by $5.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5u)\\cdot v & =5(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v),$ we compute $v\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v) & =\\frac{1}{2}(v\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}+{v}_{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4+4+0+25) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 34 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(5u)\\cdot v}{v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v)}=-\\frac{5}{17}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-139948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-34"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{17}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{34}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{25}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{17}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=27,$ then $\\frac{(2u)\\cdot u}{u\\cdot (3v)}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $(2u)\\cdot u,$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2u)\\cdot u & =2(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (3v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $3.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (3v) & =3(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =3({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3(-6-6-1-6-6) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-25) \\\\  & =-75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(2u)\\cdot u}{u\\cdot (3v)}=\\frac{54}{-75}=-\\frac{18}{25}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-74040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-75"] ["math" "-\\frac{18}{25}"] ["math" "-\\frac{27}{25}"] ["math" "-\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "54"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{18}{25}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=30,$ then $\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3}u)\\cdot u}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v)}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $(\\frac{2}{3}u)\\cdot u,$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $\\frac{2}{3}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{2}{3}u)\\cdot u & =\\frac{2}{3}(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 30 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v) & =\\frac{1}{2}(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2+0-3-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3}u)\\cdot u}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v)}=\\frac{20}{-2}=-10.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-74039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "30"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $(2a+5b)\\cdot c+(b+3c)\\cdot d,$ if $c\\cdot a=2,$ $b\\cdot c=3,$ $b\\cdot d=4$ and $d\\cdot c=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2a+5b)\\cdot c+(b+3c)\\cdot d & =(2a)\\cdot c+(5b)\\cdot c+b\\cdot d+(3c)\\cdot d \\\\  & =2(a\\cdot c)+5(b\\cdot c)+b\\cdot d+3(c\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(a\\cdot c)+5(b\\cdot c)+b\\cdot d+3(c\\cdot d) & =2(c\\cdot a)+5(b\\cdot c)+b\\cdot d+3(d\\cdot c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $c\\cdot a,$ $b\\cdot c,$ $b\\cdot d,$ and $d\\cdot c,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(c\\cdot a)+5(b\\cdot c)+b\\cdot d+3(d\\cdot c) & =2(2)+5(3)+4+3(1) \\\\  & =4+15+4+3 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(2a+5b)\\cdot c+(b+3c)\\cdot d=26.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-39196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "23"] ["math" "26"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "26"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $\\alpha \\beta =-8$ and $b\\cdot a=\\frac{15}{2}$, then what is $(\\alpha a)\\cdot (\\beta b)+12?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in both variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha a)\\cdot (\\beta b)+12 & =\\alpha (a\\cdot \\beta b)+12 \\\\  & =\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using that the dot product is symmetric, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)+12 & =\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\alpha \\beta $ and $b\\cdot a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)+12 & =(-8)\\cdot \\frac{15}{2}+12 \\\\  & =-60+12 \\\\  & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-139951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-48"] ["math" "-56"] ["math" "-60"] ["math" "40"] ["math" "72"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-48"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d,$ if $c\\cdot a=-2,$ $c\\cdot b=4,$ and $d\\cdot b=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d & =(3a)\\cdot c-(4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d \\\\  & =3(a\\cdot c)-4(b\\cdot c)+2(b\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(a\\cdot c)-4(b\\cdot c)+2(b\\cdot d) & =3(c\\cdot a)-4(c\\cdot b)+2(d\\cdot b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $c\\cdot a,$ $c\\cdot b$ and $d\\cdot b,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(c\\cdot a)-4(c\\cdot b)+2(d\\cdot b) & =3(-2)-4(4)+2(5) \\\\  & =-6-16+10 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2d)\\cdot b=-12.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-74041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10"] ["math" "-12"] ["math" "-22"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "12"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-74041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $(3a+b)\\cdot c+(2b-2c)\\cdot d,$ if $c\\cdot a=3,$ $c\\cdot b=-2,$ $b\\cdot d=-1,$ and $d\\cdot c=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3a+b)\\cdot c+(2b-2c)\\cdot d & =(3a)\\cdot c+b\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d-(2c)\\cdot d \\\\  & =3(a\\cdot c)+b\\cdot c+2(b\\cdot d)-2(c\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(a\\cdot c)+b\\cdot c+2(b\\cdot d)-2(c\\cdot d) & =3(c\\cdot a)+c\\cdot b+2(b\\cdot d)-2(d\\cdot c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $c\\cdot a,$ $c\\cdot b,$ $b\\cdot d,$ and $d\\cdot c,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(c\\cdot a)+c\\cdot b+2(b\\cdot d)-2(d\\cdot c) & =3(3)+(-2)+2(-1)-2(2) \\\\  & =9-2-2-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(3a+b)\\cdot c+(2b-2c)\\cdot d=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-39202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $\\alpha \\beta =-5$ and $a\\cdot b=3$, then what is $(\\alpha b)\\cdot (\\beta a)-9?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in both variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha b)\\cdot (\\beta a)-9 & =\\alpha (b\\cdot \\beta a)-9 \\\\  & =\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using that the dot product is symmetric, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)-9 & =\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\alpha \\beta $ and $a\\cdot b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)-9 & =(-5)\\cdot (3)-9 \\\\  & =-15-9 \\\\  & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-40049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-24"] ["math" "-30"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "48"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-24"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $b\\cdot a>0$\n- $b\\cdot a=0$\n- $a\\cdot a=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $a\\ne 0,$ we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that none of the statements are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-39203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-39203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the statements are true"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $(6a-9b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a>0$\n- $a\\cdot a=\\frac{1}{2}(b\\cdot a)$\n- $b\\cdot a=-4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-40116" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indedd, $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Simplifying the expression $(6a-9b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6a-9b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ (6a)\\cdot a-(9b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a)-9(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a) & =9(b\\cdot a) \\\\ a\\cdot a & =\\frac{3}{2}(b\\cdot a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since it leads to a contradiction. If $b\\cdot a=-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6a-9b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a)-9(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a)-9(-4) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a & =-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut this is a contradiction since $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_