e true\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cbcd6ef36778884ab630315463abd36bcdeb577db51f2b607199560a788a8240"} {"question" "q-139957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "q-139957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the statements are true"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/state.json" "file_sha256" "e4876d8775b8a0e6807996278b34b2b1a89e06bcfb58cf6c739679b9762979c1"} {"question" "e-5907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/assets/8d9347b89864ea011c9dd3fbd17b94f05c479ff7a96343aae7928b241491c3ba.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/assets/b5521921f5997e3cb748c518a9823574fcdfb82cd4bb1d1f7bbbf17d2f9ac06a.png) and $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 34,$ then what is $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/example-5907.json" "file_sha256" "8a6312c029c09f7d7d338c1f2a7c17a516451ceb72a013eafedbff5539373c85"} {"question" "e-5907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot 2\\mathbf{v},$ we compute $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ first and then multiply the result by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot 2v & =2(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =2({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =2(7\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2+(-9)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2(7+10+45-6) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 56 \\\\  & =112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $3\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we multiply $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ by $3.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3v\\cdot v & =3(v\\cdot v) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 34 \\\\  & =102.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}} =\\dfrac{112}{102}=\\dfrac{56}{51}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/example-5907.json" "file_sha256" "8a6312c029c09f7d7d338c1f2a7c17a516451ceb72a013eafedbff5539373c85"} {"question" "e-5536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d},$ if $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 2,$ $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} = -1,$ $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 2,$ and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/example-5536.json" "file_sha256" "677830618fae5090fcc35f56e471a5957a275da0cb725c5e439adfe480e0b356"} {"question" "e-5536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5a+3b)\\cdot c+(4b+2c)\\cdot d & =(5a)\\cdot c+(3b)\\cdot c+(4b)\\cdot d+(2c)\\cdot d \\\\  & =5(a\\cdot c)+3(b\\cdot c)+4(b\\cdot d)+2(c\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can substitute the values for $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d},$ and use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(a\\cdot c)+3(b\\cdot c)+4(b\\cdot d)+2(c\\cdot d) & =5(c\\cdot a)+3(-1)+4(2)+2(d\\cdot c) \\\\  & =5(c\\cdot a)-3+8+2(d\\cdot c) \\\\  & =5(c\\cdot a)+5+2(d\\cdot c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(c\\cdot a)+5+2(d\\cdot c) & =5(2)+5+2(3) \\\\  & =10+5+6 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 21.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/example-5536.json" "file_sha256" "677830618fae5090fcc35f56e471a5957a275da0cb725c5e439adfe480e0b356"} {"question" "e-5537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0},$ where $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = -2(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a})$\n\n- $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} =  0$\n\n- $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/example-5537.json" "file_sha256" "25847cea9a437f909ab4e4ba212a77c82e42a2d66b3126e0d3128c48d60d25bc"} {"question" "e-5537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4a+2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ (4a)\\cdot a+(2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 4(a\\cdot a)+2(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 2(b\\cdot a) & =-4(a\\cdot a) \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-2(a\\cdot a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ we must have $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false since it leads to a contradiction. Simplifying $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0}$ and substituting in $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4a+2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 4a\\cdot a+2b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 4a\\cdot a+2(1) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut this is a contradiction since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ implies that $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/example-5537.json" "file_sha256" "25847cea9a437f909ab4e4ba212a77c82e42a2d66b3126e0d3128c48d60d25bc"}] "usage" {"q-39205" [] "q-39202" [] "e-5536" [] "e-5537" [] "e-5907" []} "basis" 1651 "retractions" [[17592186134124 :question/problem "If $u = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$, and $u \\cdot u = 15$, then $(u \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u))/(u \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v)) =$"] [17592186134122 :question/problem "Find $(3a + b) \\cdot c + (2b - 2c) \\cdot d$, if $c \\cdot a = 3$, $c \\cdot b =-2$, $b \\cdot d =-1$, and $d \\cdot c = 2$."] [17592186133551 :question/problem "If $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 34$, then what is $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}}$?"] [17592186133551 :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}$, we compute $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ first and then multiply the result by $2$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot 2v &= 2(u \\cdot v) \\\\\n&= 2(u_{1} \\cdot v_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot v_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot v_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot v_{4}) \\\\\n&= 2(7 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 2 + (-9) \\cdot (-5) + 3 \\cdot (-2)) \\\\\n&= 2(7 + 10 + 45 - 6) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 56 \\\\\n&= 112\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $3\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$, we multiply $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ by $3$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3v \\cdot v &= 3(v \\cdot v) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 34 \\\\\n&= 102\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}} =\\dfrac{112}{102}=\\dfrac{56}{51}\n$$"] [17592186133487 :question/problem "Find $(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d}$, if $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 2$, $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} = -1$, $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 2$, and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}=3$."] [17592186133487 :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5a + 3b) \\cdot c + (4b + 2c) \\cdot d &= (5a) \\cdot c + (3b) \\cdot c + (4b) \\cdot d + (2c) \\cdot d \\\\\n&= 5(a \\cdot c) + 3(b \\cdot c) + 4(b \\cdot d) + 2(c \\cdot d)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can substitute the values for $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d}$, and use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(a \\cdot c) + 3(b \\cdot c) + 4(b \\cdot d) + 2(c \\cdot d) &= 5(c \\cdot a) + 3(-1) + 4(2) + 2(d \\cdot c) \\\\\n&= 5(c \\cdot a) - 3 + 8 + 2(d \\cdot c) \\\\\n&= 5(c \\cdot a) + 5 + 2(d \\cdot c)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}$, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(c \\cdot a) + 5 + 2(d \\cdot c) &= 5(2) + 5 + 2(3) \\\\\n&= 10 + 5 + 6 \\\\\n&= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 21\n$$"] [17592186133488 :question/problem "Suppose $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0}$, where $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = -2(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a})$\n2. $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 0$\n3. $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1$"] [17592186133488 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Simplifying, we have\n$(4a + 2b) \\cdot a|= 0; (4a) \\cdot a + (2b) \\cdot a|= 0; 4(a \\cdot a) + 2(b \\cdot a)|= 0; 2(b \\cdot a)|=-4(a \\cdot a); b \\cdot a|=-2(a \\cdot a)$.\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$, we must have $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0$.\n- Statement III is false since it leads to a contradiction. Simplifying $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0}$ and substituting in $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1$, we get\n$(4a + 2b) \\cdot a|= 0; 4a \\cdot a + 2b \\cdot a|= 0; 4a \\cdot a + 2(1)|= 0; a \\cdot a|=-\\frac{1}{2}$,\nbut this is a contradiction since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ implies that $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"I only\"."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131175 17592186131176 17592186131177 1759218613117